## Why This Is Asked
Tests a novel fixed-gap non-crossing pairing constraint, a natural extension of classic non-crossing matching problems, while probing DP state design and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes with fixed pair distance
- Non-crossing/disjoint constraint handling
- Backpointers for reconstruction
- Space/time optimization trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxFixedGapPairs(A, d, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const take = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(false));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      if (t > 0 && i - d - 1 >= 0) {
        const cand = dp[i - d - 1][t - 1] + A[i - 1] * A[i - d - 1];
        if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = cand;
          take[i][t] = true;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    if (take[i][t]) {
      pairs.push([i - d, i]); // 1-based indices of the pair
      i = i - d - 1;
      t--;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }
  pairs.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to generalize DP with an extra resource dimension and reconstruction, a common pattern in grid path problems with constraints. It also probes edge-case handling when i or j is at 0 and when t=0. 

## Key Concepts
- DP with an extra dimension for resource usage (K diagonals)
- 3-way transitions and path reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPath(grid, K) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(INF)));
  const parent = Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array.from({ length: m }, () => Array(K+1).fill(null)));
  dp[0][0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0 && t === 0) continue;
        let best = INF, pick = null;
        if (i > 0 && dp[i-1][j][t] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j][t]; pick = [i-1, j, t]; }
        if (j > 0 && dp[i][j-1][t] < best) { best = dp[i][j-1][t]; pick = [i, j-1, t]; }
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 && t > 0 && dp[i-1][j-1][t-1] < best) { best = dp[i-1][j-1][t-1]; pick = [i-1, j-1, t-1]; }
        if (best < INF) {
          dp[i][j][t] = best + grid[i][j];
          if (pick) parent[i][j][t] = pick;
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // find best end state
  let bestCost = INF, endT = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= K; t++) {
    if (dp[n-1][m-1][t] < bestCost) { bestCost = dp[n-1][m-1][t]; endT = t; }
  }
  // reconstruct path
  const path = [];
  let i = n-1, j = m-1, t = endT;
  while (i !== 0 || j !== 0 || t !== 0) {
    path.push([i, j]);
    const p = parent[i][j][t];
    if (!p) break;
    [i, j, t] = p;
  }
  path.push([0,0]);
  path.reverse();
  return { cost: bestCost, path };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to reduce space to O(m K) using rolling arrays?
- How would you handle negative costs or unreachable endpoints?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP formulation for selecting fixed-count non-overlapping subarrays with a per-block cost, plus reconstruction of blocks.

## Key Concepts

- DP over i (prefix) and t (blocks)
- Prefix sums for subarray sums
- Running best to avoid O(n^2)
- Reconstructing start/end indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxKSubarraysWithCost(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) pref[i+1] = pref[i] + A[i];
  const NEG = -1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(NEG));
  const from = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => new Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  const bestVal = new Array(k+1).fill(NEG);
  const bestIdx = new Array(k+1).fill(-1);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++){
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++){
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      from[i][t] = {type:'skip', at:i-1, t};
    }
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++){
      const val = dp[i-1][t-1] - pref[i-1];
      if (val > bestVal[t]) { bestVal[t] = val; bestIdx[t] = i-1; }
      const cand = bestVal[t] + pref[i] - c;
      if (cand > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = cand;
        from[i][t] = {type:'end', end:i-1, start:bestIdx[t], t};
      }
    }
  }
  let i = n, t = k;
  const blocks = [];
  while (i > 0 && t > 0){
    const info = from[i][t];
    if (!info) break;
    if (info.type === 'end'){
      blocks.push([info.start, info.end]);
      i = info.start;
      t--;
    } else {
      i = info.at;
    }
  }
  blocks.reverse();
  return { value: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for at-most-k blocks?
- How would you handle negative values of c or allow zero-cost blocks?
- What modifications optimize space to O(k) while preserving reconstruction?

